Patches next 16.4.2 in two places, each in both its CommonJS and ESM copies.

1. The "use cache" wrapper.

Defect: concurrent requests for the same cache key share one in-progress fill, and a request that
joins another's fill takes its result without checking it against the tags that expired after the
fill started. The leader path runs those checks only on a cache handler's entry and on reuse within
one request. So a recipe list read that starts just after a delete's updateTag can join a fill that
began before the delete and show the deleted recipe for one response; create and edit, which call
updateTag and then redirect, share the window. Upstream canary has the same code.

Fix: before using a joined fill, run the leader path's checks in its order (refresh the handler's
tags, read the implicit tags' expiration, shouldDiscardCacheEntry). A built-in handler is then asked
for each of the fill's tags' expiration, since its getExpiration reads the tag state its get()
checks; an expiration after the fill started and not in the future sends the request round the loop
to regenerate. Any other handler, such as Vercel's for "use cache: remote", may report Infinity or 0
and leave tags to get(), as the cacheHandlers contract allows, so it judges the fill through its own
get(), as the leader path does on a hit: the fill, or an older entry carrying its tags, passing get()
is used, and a miss, a rejection or a newer entry regenerates. The cost is one extra fill for each
joiner that races a tag expiration on a built-in handler. On any other handler it is one handler
read for each joiner, and one extra fill for each read that misses or finds a newer entry, for any
cause: an eviction or a failed write as well as a tag expiration.
Under next dev, a custom handler is read through Next's tiered handler, whose get() answers from
its in-memory front before the custom one has judged the entry, so there the guard can still take an
outdated fill; a deployment reads its handler directly, and this project configures none of its own.

Remove this part once a Next release fixes vercel/next.js#100345; running that issue's reproduction
against the new version shows whether it does.

2. The dev server's prerender manifest, under next dev only.

Defect: DevServer.getStaticPaths adds a dynamic route's static paths to
.next/dev/prerender-manifest.json with an awaited readFile, then a truncating writeFile, and no lock,
so /recipes/[slug] and its cooking view, resolving together, interleave their updates. A shorter
write over a longer one leaves the longer one's tail after valid JSON, and every page then fails to
parse the manifest until next dev restarts; a request that reads during a write finds the file
empty. Upstream canary has the same code.

Fix: read, update and write the manifest without yielding, writing it with Next's own
writeFileAtomic (a temporary file renamed over the manifest), so updates never interleave and a
reader sees a whole file. The cost is a synchronous read of the manifest, and a write when it
changes, on next dev's event loop each time a dynamic route's static paths resolve.

Remove this part once a Next release fixes vercel/next.js#96259, whose fix is open as
vercel/next.js#96384.

Remove the patch and its patchedDependencies entry in pnpm-workspace.yaml once both parts are gone.
The entry names next@16.4.2, so changing next's version fails pnpm install until the patch is
re-made against the new version or removed, and re-making it with pnpm patch drops this header, so
carry it over.

